TY  - JOUR
TI  - Epistemic problems in Hayek’s defence of free markets
AU  - Benson, Jonathan
PY  - 2024
T2  - Economics and Philosophy
AB  - Friedrich von Hayek’s classical liberalism argued that free markets allow individuals the greatest opportunity to achieve their ends. This paper develops an internal critique of this claim. It argues that once externalities are introduced, the forms of economic knowledge Hayek thought to undermine government action and orthodox utilitarianism also rule out relative welfarist assessments of more or less regulated markets. Given the pervasiveness of externalities in modern economies, Hayek will frequently be unable to make comparative welfarist claims, or he must relax his epistemic assumptions and allow for greater government action than his classical liberalism would wish to accept.
DO  - 10.1017/s0266267124000105
